The code statements that lead to the bug are:
nvm->nregions = n; /* in case where kasprintf fail */
if (!name)
    continue;
Reason:
The assignment of `nvm->nregions` occurs after the loop that populates `nvm->regions[].` Before that assignment, `nvm->nregions` is zero. Because `regions` is a flexible array member whose accessible size is logically tied to `nregions`, accessing `nvm->regions[0]` before setting `nregions` causes UBSAN to treat the array as zero‑length, leading to an out‑of‑bounds error. Additionally, silently ignoring a `kasprintf` failure with `continue` leaves an uninitialized region entry and results in an incorrect final value of `nregions`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
nvm->nregions = nregions;
if (!name) {
    ret = -ENOMEM;
    goto err;
}
Reason:
Setting `nvm->nregions` immediately after allocating the struct ensures that the flexible array size is known before any element is written, eliminating the UBSAN warning. Replacing the `continue` with an explicit error path (`goto err`) ensures that `kasprintf` failures are handled properly – the function will clean up allocated resources and return an error instead of leaving the structure in an inconsistent state with a mismatched `nregions` count.

